[Chapter "Classical Variation"]
[Line "Quiet 6.Nf3"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation"]

{White skips h4. The same ideas work: control e5, trade the light-squared bishops, finish developing.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. Nc3 dxe4 {Take on e4.} 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Develop with tempo.} 5. Ng3 Bg6 {Stay on the diagonal.} 6. Nf3 Nd7 {Again, control e5 before White's knight lands there.} 7. Bd3 Bxd3 {Trade the bishops; it takes the sting out of White's kingside play.} 8. Qxd3 e6 {Close the pawn chain and free the f8 bishop.} 9. O-O Ngf6 {Develop.} 10. Re1 Be7 {Ready to castle, with a very solid position.} *
